  <main class="detail-main" id="main" tabindex="-1"><div class="detail-hero"><div class="wrap detail-hero-inner"><nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/journal/">Journal</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Make your next preventive visit count</span></nav><p class="eyebrow">Prevention · 4 min read</p><h1>Make your next preventive visit count</h1><p class="detail-lead">A preventive visit is time to compare what has changed, check how daily care is working and plan what comes next.</p></div></div><div class="wrap detail-grid"><article class="detail-card prose"><p class="editorial-note" data-editorial-review>Staging only — this article is hidden from the public site until clinically approved in the Raina Website settings.</p><section><h2>Bring what has changed</h2><p>Mention new medications, changes in your health and anything you have noticed about your teeth or gums. Ask what the dentist checks during the exam and which findings are worth revisiting over time.</p></section><section><h2>Ask for one practical demonstration</h2><p>If a space is hard to clean, ask the team to show you how to reach it. Discuss which brush or interdental tool fits the area and how to incorporate it into the routine you already follow.</p></section><section><h2>Plan the next visit while you are there</h2><p>Ask what follow-up interval makes sense for your needs and what the next visit is meant to accomplish. If you are considering an in-house membership, compare its included visits and written terms with the care the dentist has recommended.</p></section><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a class="text-link" href="https://www.mouthhealthy.org/oral-health-recommendations">ADA MouthHealthy · Oral health recommendations <svg class="icon" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-arrow"/></svg></a></li></ul><a class="text-link" href="/care/membership/">Compare in-house membership plans <svg class="icon" aria-